The function validates each element and scales the sum to reduce the risk of overflow with very large finite values.

```javascript
function mean(values) {
  if (!Array.isArray(values) || values.length === 0) return null;
  let scale = 0;
  for (const value of values) {
    if (!Number.isFinite(value)) return null;
    scale = Math.max(scale, Math.abs(value));
  }
  if (scale === 0) return 0;
  return (values.reduce((sum, value) => sum + value / scale, 0)
    / values.length) * scale;
}

console.log(mean([2, 4, 6])); // 4
console.log(mean([]));        // null
console.log(mean([Infinity]));// null
```

As with ordinary JavaScript number arithmetic, results are subject to floating-point rounding.
